The discrepancy between the dates you observe in your code and in Windows Explorer is due to the fact that Windows uses a different time zone for file timestamps. By default, Windows stores file times in local time, not UTC. Therefore, when you convert the FILETIME to a `DateTime` using `DateTime.FromFileTimeUtc()`, you are effectively converting it to UTC time, which may differ from the local time displayed in Windows Explorer.

To resolve this issue, you should convert the FILETIME to a `DateTime` using `DateTime.FromFileTime()`, which will correctly interpret the FILETIME as local time. Here's how you can modify your method:

```csharp
private static DateTime convertToDateTime(System.Runtime.InteropServices.ComTypes.FILETIME time)
{
    long combinedTime = time.dwHighDateTime << 32 | time.dwLowDateTime;
    // Convert to local time
    return DateTime.FromFileTime(combinedTime);
}
```

This should give you the same date as the one displayed in Windows Explorer. Remember that this approach assumes that the FILETIME is stored in local time, which is the default behavior in Windows. If you need to work with UTC times, you will need to adjust your approach accordingly

