This is due to the way php handles variables, and is a bit counter-intuitive to anyone who has worked in C or C++. Passing by reference to be smarter than PHP isn't advised. PHP doesn't actually make copies of data unless it needs to (i.e. you change a variable's value when there's more than 1 reference to it), an optimization strategy very similar to copy-on-write for shared memory pages. So, let's say you have a variable that you pass by value several times in a given script. If you then take this variable and pass it by reference, you're actually duplicating the variable rather than just getting a pointer to the object.

