Data Abstraction, on the other hand, is about capturing data patterns, abstracting over data. In Java, this is typically done with classes. For example, you could have a class like this:

public class Point {
    private int x;
    private int y;

    public Point(int x, int y) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
    }

    public int getX() {
        return x;
    }

    public int getY() {
        return y;
    }
}

In this case, users can use the Point class without having to worry about how the data is stored or implemented. They can create new Points, get the x and y coordinates of existing Points, etc. This is data abstraction because it abstracts over the data, not the behaviour.

In summary, Procedural Abstraction is about abstracting over behaviour (methods), while Data Abstraction is about abstracting over data (classes). In Java, both are typically implemented as part of classes, but they serve different purposes. Procedural Abstraction is about encapsulating behaviour, while Data Abstraction is about encapsulating data.

