In ASP.NET MVC, the DefaultValue attribute is not used to set a default value for a property in a view model. Instead, you can use a constructor or an initializer method to set default values. Here's an example using a constructor:

```csharp
public class SearchModel
{
    public SearchModel()
    {
        IsMale = true;
        IsFemale = true;
    }

    public bool IsMale { get; set; }
    public bool IsFemale { get; set; }
}
```

Alternatively, you can use an initializer method:

```csharp
public class SearchModel
{
    public SearchModel()
    {
        Initialize();
    }

    private void Initialize()
    {
        IsMale = true;
        IsFemale = true;
    }

    public bool IsMale { get; set; }
    public bool IsFemale { get; set; }
}
```

Both of these methods ensure that the properties have default values when the model is instantiated. This approach is more straightforward and easier to understand than using attributes or private fields.

