However, this approach has its own problems. If the function takes a long time to execute, the next scheduled execution might be missed. This could lead to a significant delay in the execution of the function. To avoid this, we can use a different approach by using the performance API, which provides a high resolution timer.

Here's how you can use it:

```javascript
var start = performance.now();
var f = function() {
    var elapsed = performance.now() - start;
    var drift = elapsed % 1000;
    $('<li>').text(drift + "ms").appendTo('#results');

    // Schedule the next execution based on the elapsed time
    setTimeout(f, 1000 - drift);
};

f();
```

This approach ensures that the function is called as close to every 1000ms as possible, even if the previous execution took longer than expected. However, keep in mind that the performance API is not supported in all browsers, so you may need to provide a fallback for older browsers.

