However, method overloading is resolved at compile time, not at runtime. The compiler looks at the declared type of the variable, not the runtime type. In your case, the declared type of `b` is `A`, so the compiler chooses the method that takes an `A` as a parameter. If you want to use the method that takes a `B`, you need to cast `b` to `B`:

    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        var a = new A();
        // Call first method
        MyMethod(a);

        A b = new B();
        // Call second method
        MyMethod((B)b);

        Console.ReadLine();
    }

This will work as expected, because the compiler will now choose the method that takes a `B` as a parameter, since `b` is being explicitly cast to `B`.

